From: Eugene Feinberg <uge@persimmons.ai>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 20:12:10 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 18/37] comb.extract canonicalizer: do not self-replace through
 a concat cycle

In a graph region the concat may (transitively) use the very extract being
canonicalized: %e = extract(%c); %c = concat(..., %e, ...) is how a
partial-update false loop looks after aggregates are lowered to bit vectors
(a struct_inject chain over a probe of the signal being driven, once
flattened). isOpTriviallyRecursive only guards DIRECT self-operands; when
extractConcatToConcatExtract selects exactly the extract's own result out of
the concat, the 'replacement' IS the op -- RAUW of x with x leaves the uses
in place and RewriterBase::eraseOp asserts use_empty.

Bail instead; there is nothing to simplify. Found on CVA6 after the
struct_inject lowering landed: canonicalize crashed on 58 modules' worth of
false-loop shapes that previously never reached this pattern.
---
 lib/Dialect/Comb/CombFolds.cpp | 8 ++++++++
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)

diff --git a/lib/Dialect/Comb/CombFolds.cpp b/lib/Dialect/Comb/CombFolds.cpp
index d80b0cab2..e5b9967d1 100644
--- a/lib/Dialect/Comb/CombFolds.cpp
+++ b/lib/Dialect/Comb/CombFolds.cpp
@@ -576,6 +576,14 @@ extractConcatToConcatExtract(ExtractOp op, ConcatOp innerCat,
   }
 
   if (reverseConcatArgs.size() == 1) {
+    // In a graph region the concat may (transitively) use this very extract:
+    // `%e = extract(%c); %c = concat(..., %e, ...)` is how a partial-update
+    // false loop looks after aggregates are lowered to bit vectors. If the
+    // extract selects exactly its own result out of the concat, the
+    // "replacement" IS the op -- a self-RAUW leaves the uses in place and
+    // eraseOp asserts. Bail; there is nothing to simplify.
+    if (reverseConcatArgs[0] == op.getResult())
+      return failure();
     replaceOpAndCopyNamehint(rewriter, op, reverseConcatArgs[0]);
   } else {
     replaceOpWithNewOpAndCopyNamehint<ConcatOp>(
